1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:07,252
Welcome to the first chapter of my study series on the book Build Your First LLM, by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:07,252 --> 00:00:18,526
I'm making this series for myself as much as for you: the goal is to understand transformers properly, all the way down to the tensor shapes, and then build one.

3
00:00:18,526 --> 00:00:21,117
This first chapter is the foundation.

4
00:00:21,117 --> 00:00:21,957
No code yet.

5
00:00:21,957 --> 00:00:33,652
We're going to clear away the myths, and replace them with a precise mental model of what an AI system actually is, how it learns, and where large language models fit.

6
00:00:34,502 --> 00:00:37,003
A quick note on how to use these videos.

7
00:00:37,003 --> 00:00:43,696
Every chapter has a slide deck, long-form study notes, and links into the book's companion code repository.

8
00:00:43,696 --> 00:00:48,262
The first four chapters are conceptual, so there is no notebook for them.

9
00:00:48,262 --> 00:00:52,452
From chapter five onward we'll be writing and running real PyTorch.

10
00:00:52,452 --> 00:00:57,894
These videos are a teaching adaptation in my own words, not a replacement for the book.

11
00:00:57,894 --> 00:01:02,022
If you find them useful, please buy the book, it goes much deeper.

12
00:01:02,871 --> 00:01:04,216
Here's the roadmap.

13
00:01:04,216 --> 00:01:07,967
We start by throwing out the Hollywood picture of AI.

14
00:01:07,967 --> 00:01:13,630
Then we define AI properly as pattern recognition, using the humble spam filter.

15
00:01:13,630 --> 00:01:24,741
We'll write down the simple formula behind modern AI, and then look very concretely at what learning means, by watching a model fit a line, number by number.

16
00:01:24,741 --> 00:01:38,189
After that we place large language models in the family tree of AI, look at a short history, and then get honest about what language models can and cannot do, including why they hallucinate.

17
00:01:38,189 --> 00:01:46,258
We finish with exercises, the reasons to build one yourself, the key takeaways, and review questions with answers.

18
00:01:47,108 --> 00:01:53,794
Let me put the vocabulary on the table first, because we'll use these words for the rest of the series.

19
00:01:53,794 --> 00:01:56,780
Artificial intelligence is the broad umbrella.

20
00:01:56,780 --> 00:02:01,518
Machine learning is the part of AI where the rules are learned from data.

21
00:02:01,518 --> 00:02:06,517
Deep learning is machine learning with neural networks that have many layers.

22
00:02:06,517 --> 00:02:13,333
A large language model is a deep network trained on an enormous amount of text to predict the next token.

23
00:02:13,333 --> 00:02:17,617
Parameters, also called weights, are the numbers inside the model.

24
00:02:17,617 --> 00:02:20,928
Training is the process of adjusting those numbers.

25
00:02:20,928 --> 00:02:27,354
Emergence is when surprisingly complex skills appear from a simple training goal once you scale up.

26
00:02:27,354 --> 00:02:33,651
And hallucination is when the model produces something fluent and confident that is simply false.

27
00:02:34,501 --> 00:02:35,363
Section one.

28
00:02:35,363 --> 00:02:36,655
Forget the movies.

29
00:02:37,506 --> 00:02:44,602
Films give us killer robots, a calm computer voice that turns on its crew, and androids that fall in love.

30
00:02:44,602 --> 00:02:49,222
Great stories, but almost nothing to do with the systems we'll build.

31
00:02:49,222 --> 00:02:53,975
Read the right column carefully, because each line is a technical fact.

32
00:02:53,975 --> 00:02:57,724
Today's systems show no evidence of feelings or desires.

33
00:02:57,724 --> 00:02:59,398
They don't want anything.

34
00:02:59,398 --> 00:03:01,407
They process patterns in data.

35
00:03:01,407 --> 00:03:04,486
They are limited by what they were trained on.

36
00:03:04,486 --> 00:03:10,646
And physically, the whole thing is a lot of matrix multiplication running on graphics cards.

37
00:03:10,646 --> 00:03:16,270
The last line, sophisticated autocomplete, sounds like an insult, but hold on to it.

38
00:03:16,270 --> 00:03:21,425
It is literally the training objective of the model we'll build in this book.

39
00:03:22,275 --> 00:03:25,199
Here's what autocomplete means concretely.

40
00:03:25,199 --> 00:03:32,581
Give the model the words, the cat sat on the, and it produces a probability for every possible next token.

41
00:03:32,581 --> 00:03:40,588
In this illustrative chart, mat gets about forty six percent, floor twenty one, sofa seventeen, and moon only four.

42
00:03:40,588 --> 00:03:43,722
The model picks one, appends it, and repeats.

43
00:03:43,722 --> 00:03:46,089
That's the entire generation loop.

44
00:03:46,089 --> 00:03:54,445
What's remarkable is that training only on this one simple task produces systems that can write essays and working code.

45
00:03:54,445 --> 00:04:01,757
But don't lose the mechanical picture: it is pattern matching over text, extremely good pattern matching.

46
00:04:02,607 --> 00:04:06,332
The book quotes the computer scientist Edsger Dijkstra here.

47
00:04:06,332 --> 00:04:12,044
His point is that asking whether a computer thinks is like asking whether a submarine swims.

48
00:04:12,044 --> 00:04:16,514
A submarine doesn't move like a fish, but it clearly gets through water.

49
00:04:16,514 --> 00:04:23,405
In the same way, these models don't think the way we do, but they process information in genuinely useful ways.

50
00:04:23,405 --> 00:04:28,868
As builders, we care about what the system does and how it does it, not about the label.

51
00:04:29,718 --> 00:04:30,702
Section two.

52
00:04:30,702 --> 00:04:32,833
AI is pattern recognition.

53
00:04:33,683 --> 00:04:35,483
Here's a working definition.

54
00:04:35,483 --> 00:04:37,733
AI is pattern recognition at scale.

55
00:04:37,733 --> 00:04:47,762
It finds patterns in data that are too complicated, or simply too many, for a person to write down as rules, and it uses those patterns to make predictions.

56
00:04:47,762 --> 00:04:49,434
The words at scale matter.

57
00:04:49,434 --> 00:04:55,220
We're talking about millions of examples and millions, or billions, of adjustable numbers.

58
00:04:55,220 --> 00:05:02,099
The best everyday example is something you use every day without thinking about it, your email spam filter.

59
00:05:02,949 --> 00:05:05,636
Look at the two halves of this diagram.

60
00:05:05,636 --> 00:05:08,323
On the left is traditional programming.

61
00:05:08,323 --> 00:05:14,869
A human writes the rules, for example, if the email contains a certain phrase, mark it as spam.

62
00:05:14,869 --> 00:05:19,279
Data goes in with those rules, and the program produces answers.

63
00:05:19,279 --> 00:05:21,553
On the right is machine learning.

64
00:05:21,553 --> 00:05:22,793
We flip it around.

65
00:05:22,793 --> 00:05:30,855
We give the system data together with the answers, emails labelled spam or not spam, and training produces the rules.

66
00:05:30,855 --> 00:05:34,506
The learned rules are not lines of code anyone wrote.

67
00:05:34,506 --> 00:05:36,987
They are patterns stored as numbers.

68
00:05:36,987 --> 00:05:40,639
That inversion is the whole idea of machine learning.

69
00:05:41,489 --> 00:05:42,370
Step by step.

70
00:05:42,370 --> 00:05:48,133
First, training data: a huge pile of emails, each labelled by a human as spam or not.

71
00:05:48,133 --> 00:05:51,116
Second, the system finds patterns by itself.

72
00:05:51,116 --> 00:06:02,032
It might notice that spam uses lots of capital letters, talks about money, prizes and urgency, comes from certain kinds of addresses, and has unusual formatting.

73
00:06:02,032 --> 00:06:09,083
Third, when a new email arrives, it scores that email against all those patterns and makes a prediction.

74
00:06:09,083 --> 00:06:13,490
The crucial point is that no programmer typed any of those rules.

75
00:06:13,490 --> 00:06:15,524
They came out of the examples.

76
00:06:15,524 --> 00:06:27,456
The book compares this to training a new employee to sort mail: you don't hand them a five hundred page rulebook, you go through a stack together until they get the hang of it.

77
00:06:28,306 --> 00:06:33,167
Here's a toy picture of what learned patterns look like inside a model.

78
00:06:33,167 --> 00:06:35,973
Each word gets a number, called a weight.

79
00:06:35,973 --> 00:06:41,518
Positive weights push towards spam, negative weights push towards a normal email.

80
00:06:41,518 --> 00:06:45,146
Free, winner, and dollar signs are strongly positive.

81
00:06:45,146 --> 00:06:47,816
Meeting, lunch and report are negative.

82
00:06:47,816 --> 00:06:52,334
To classify an email, add up the weights of the words it contains.

83
00:06:52,334 --> 00:07:03,287
For example, an email saying free lunch meeting scores two point one, minus one point five, minus one point three, which is minus zero point seven, so not spam.

84
00:07:03,287 --> 00:07:14,103
These particular numbers are made up for illustration, but the principle is real: a learned pattern is just a number, and learning means finding good numbers.

85
00:07:14,953 --> 00:07:22,036
The book boils modern AI down to a formula: statistics, plus lots of data, plus computing power.

86
00:07:22,036 --> 00:07:27,202
Statistics gives us the methods to find patterns and make predictions.

87
00:07:27,202 --> 00:07:29,194
Data gives us the examples.

88
00:07:29,194 --> 00:07:32,736
Compute lets us process them in reasonable time.

89
00:07:32,736 --> 00:07:35,244
Notice that the statistics is old.

90
00:07:35,244 --> 00:07:38,417
Many of the core ideas existed decades ago.

91
00:07:38,417 --> 00:07:46,903
What changed is that the internet gave us enormous amounts of data, and graphics processors gave us enough compute.

92
00:07:46,903 --> 00:07:51,256
That combination is why AI suddenly seems to be everywhere.

93
00:07:52,106 --> 00:07:55,030
Now the most important idea in this chapter.

94
00:07:55,030 --> 00:07:59,018
When we say a model learns, we mean something very specific.

95
00:07:59,018 --> 00:08:04,799
A model is just a big collection of numbers, its parameters, and they start out random.

96
00:08:04,799 --> 00:08:06,062
Training is a loop.

97
00:08:06,062 --> 00:08:07,590
One, make a prediction.

98
00:08:07,590 --> 00:08:09,584
Two, measure how wrong it was.

99
00:08:09,584 --> 00:08:11,976
That measurement is called the loss.

100
00:08:11,976 --> 00:08:18,024
Three, nudge every number slightly in the direction that would have made the error smaller.

101
00:08:18,024 --> 00:08:21,878
Four, repeat, for real language models, billions of times.

102
00:08:21,878 --> 00:08:22,543
That's it.

103
00:08:22,543 --> 00:08:31,315
Everything in later chapters, the attention layers, the transformer blocks, is just a more elaborate way of arranging those numbers.

104
00:08:31,315 --> 00:08:33,508
The learning loop stays the same.

105
00:08:34,358 --> 00:08:39,653
Let's make that concrete with the simplest model possible: a straight line.

106
00:08:39,653 --> 00:08:42,830
The prediction y hat equals w times x plus b.

107
00:08:42,830 --> 00:08:48,901
The weight w is the slope, and the bias b is where the line crosses the vertical axis.

108
00:08:48,901 --> 00:08:54,902
On the left, we start with w equal to four point five and b equal to zero point four.

109
00:08:54,902 --> 00:08:59,138
The green line is too steep and misses the blue data points.

110
00:08:59,138 --> 00:09:13,116
On the right, after four hundred small adjustment steps of gradient descent, which I actually ran for this video, the line fits the data with w about three point oh nine and b about one point seven.

111
00:09:13,116 --> 00:09:21,093
The data was generated from slope three and intercept two, plus noise, so the model recovered the hidden pattern.

112
00:09:21,943 --> 00:09:25,148
Here are the two numbers over the training steps.

113
00:09:25,148 --> 00:09:26,979
The gold line is the weight.

114
00:09:26,979 --> 00:09:29,857
It falls from four point five towards three.

115
00:09:29,857 --> 00:09:34,239
The mint line is the bias, rising from zero point four towards two.

116
00:09:34,239 --> 00:09:36,659
The dashed lines are the true values.

117
00:09:36,659 --> 00:09:47,843
Each step uses a simple update: take the current weight and subtract a small learning rate times the gradient, which is the slope of the error with respect to that weight.

118
00:09:47,843 --> 00:09:53,664
The gradient tells you which direction makes the error bigger, so you step the other way.

119
00:09:53,664 --> 00:10:00,466
You'll see this exact rule again in chapter twelve, applied to a hundred million numbers instead of two.

120
00:10:01,315 --> 00:10:05,554
And this is the loss, the mean squared error, on a log scale.

121
00:10:05,554 --> 00:10:10,765
It starts around ten point three and falls to about zero point eight eight.

122
00:10:10,765 --> 00:10:19,033
It can't reach zero, because the data has random noise in it, so the best possible line still misses individual points.

123
00:10:19,033 --> 00:10:25,355
That floor is a useful idea to keep: a model can only be as good as the signal in its data.

124
00:10:25,355 --> 00:10:28,412
Learning is not philosophical or mysterious.

125
00:10:28,412 --> 00:10:33,971
It is adjusting numbers based on errors, until the numbers produce good outputs.

126
00:10:34,821 --> 00:10:38,269
Put our toy next to a real large language model.

127
00:10:38,269 --> 00:10:40,281
Our line has two parameters.

128
00:10:40,281 --> 00:10:43,658
GPT three has one hundred seventy five billion.

129
00:10:43,658 --> 00:10:45,597
We trained on forty points.

130
00:10:45,597 --> 00:10:49,908
GPT three trained on hundreds of billions of tokens of text.

131
00:10:49,908 --> 00:10:52,279
Our task was predicting y from x.

132
00:10:52,279 --> 00:10:56,949
Its task is predicting the next token from all the previous ones.

133
00:10:56,949 --> 00:10:58,386
And the update rule?

134
00:10:58,386 --> 00:11:06,720
It's the same family: gradient descent, in practice with an optimiser called Adam that we'll meet in chapter twelve.

135
00:11:06,720 --> 00:11:09,953
The mechanism really does scale up unchanged.

136
00:11:09,953 --> 00:11:13,257
That's the reassuring part of this whole book.

137
00:11:14,107 --> 00:11:15,266
Section three.

138
00:11:15,266 --> 00:11:16,838
The AI family tree.

139
00:11:17,688 --> 00:11:19,767
Think of AI as nested circles.

140
00:11:19,767 --> 00:11:27,599
The outer circle is artificial intelligence, any system doing something we'd call intelligent if a person did it.

141
00:11:27,599 --> 00:11:31,619
That includes hand-written rule systems and chess engines.

142
00:11:31,619 --> 00:11:39,866
Inside it is machine learning, systems that learn from data instead of fixed rules, like spam filters and recommenders.

143
00:11:39,866 --> 00:11:44,995
Inside that is deep learning, which uses neural networks with many layers.

144
00:11:44,995 --> 00:11:53,935
And inside deep learning are large language models, deep networks specialised for language and trained to predict the next token.

145
00:11:53,935 --> 00:11:58,579
Every LLM is deep learning, which is machine learning, which is AI.

146
00:11:58,579 --> 00:12:00,519
But not the other way round.

147
00:12:01,369 --> 00:12:02,845
Why is it called deep?

148
00:12:02,845 --> 00:12:09,886
Because the network has many layers stacked on top of each other, and each layer builds on the one below.

149
00:12:09,886 --> 00:12:21,152
In image recognition, the early layers detect simple edges and curves, the middle layers combine them into parts like eyes, and the deep layers conclude this is a face.

150
00:12:21,152 --> 00:12:31,144
Language models work similarly in spirit: lower layers deal with word pieces, middle layers with phrases and grammar, and higher layers with meaning.

151
00:12:31,144 --> 00:12:38,185
In chapter eleven we'll stack twelve transformer blocks, and that stacking is exactly what makes it deep.

152
00:12:39,035 --> 00:12:40,106
A quick history.

153
00:12:40,106 --> 00:12:43,787
In nineteen fifty Alan Turing proposed his famous test.

154
00:12:43,787 --> 00:12:50,080
In nineteen fifty six the term artificial intelligence was coined at the Dartmouth conference.

155
00:12:50,080 --> 00:12:58,380
Then came early optimism and, in the sixties and seventies, the first AI winter, when progress stalled and funding dried up.

156
00:12:58,380 --> 00:13:01,660
In ninety seven Deep Blue beat Kasparov at chess.

157
00:13:01,660 --> 00:13:07,818
In twenty twelve AlexNet won the ImageNet competition and kicked off the deep learning boom.

158
00:13:07,818 --> 00:13:15,984
Then the one highlighted in gold: twenty seventeen, the paper Attention Is All You Need, which introduced the transformer.

159
00:13:15,984 --> 00:13:30,911
GPT one followed in twenty eighteen with a hundred seventeen million parameters, GPT three in twenty twenty with a hundred seventy five billion, and ChatGPT in late twenty twenty two brought all of this into the mainstream.

160
00:13:31,761 --> 00:13:35,911
The twenty seventeen paper is the reason this series exists.

161
00:13:35,911 --> 00:13:43,103
It introduced the transformer architecture, and every modern large language model is a descendant of it.

162
00:13:43,103 --> 00:13:47,045
The book builds a GPT two style transformer from scratch.

163
00:13:47,045 --> 00:13:58,042
Chapters three and four give the intuition, chapter ten implements attention in PyTorch, chapter eleven assembles the full model, and chapter twelve trains it.

164
00:13:58,042 --> 00:14:05,718
So when you get to chapter ten in this series, you'll be writing the very mechanism that this paper introduced.

165
00:14:06,568 --> 00:14:07,462
Section four.

166
00:14:07,462 --> 00:14:10,280
What makes large language models special?

167
00:14:11,130 --> 00:14:13,795
Why was language so hard for computers?

168
00:14:13,795 --> 00:14:16,118
Take the sentence, I saw her duck.

169
00:14:16,118 --> 00:14:20,490
Did you see the bird she owns, or did you see her drop her head?

170
00:14:20,490 --> 00:14:23,223
You resolve this instantly from context.

171
00:14:23,223 --> 00:14:32,583
Earlier systems struggled because they had no good way to represent context, they looked at words mostly in isolation or in tiny windows.

172
00:14:32,583 --> 00:14:34,564
The transformer changed that.

173
00:14:34,564 --> 00:14:40,986
It lets every word look at every other word in the passage and update its meaning accordingly.

174
00:14:40,986 --> 00:14:46,452
That mechanism is called attention, and it's the star of chapters three and ten.

175
00:14:47,302 --> 00:14:59,575
Modern language models can write and edit, generate code, translate, summarise, answer questions, reason through multi-step problems, though not always reliably, and adopt personas.

176
00:14:59,575 --> 00:15:04,117
The striking thing is that none of these abilities were programmed.

177
00:15:04,117 --> 00:15:07,575
Nobody wrote a translation module or a code module.

178
00:15:07,575 --> 00:15:13,542
They all came out of training on next token prediction over a huge, varied body of text.

179
00:15:14,392 --> 00:15:16,867
This phenomenon is called emergence.

180
00:15:16,867 --> 00:15:22,230
A simple objective, applied at large enough scale, produces complex behaviour.

181
00:15:22,230 --> 00:15:36,532
Nobody fully understands why predicting the next token leads to something that looks like reasoning, and researchers actively debate whether it's genuine reasoning or extremely sophisticated pattern matching.

182
00:15:36,532 --> 00:15:40,657
As a builder, my practical stance is: don't assume, measure.

183
00:15:40,657 --> 00:15:43,545
Test the model on the task you care about.

184
00:15:43,545 --> 00:15:48,840
We'll come back to evaluation when we fine-tune and deploy later in the book.

185
00:15:49,689 --> 00:15:56,328
Now the limits, and it's important to be precise about the base model, the raw network on its own.

186
00:15:56,328 --> 00:16:03,576
It has no real-time information, because its knowledge was frozen at the point training data was collected.

187
00:16:03,576 --> 00:16:09,605
It has no memory between sessions, each conversation starts from an empty context window.

188
00:16:09,605 --> 00:16:12,992
It cannot act in the world, it only produces text.

189
00:16:12,992 --> 00:16:20,444
It is unreliable at arithmetic, because it predicts digit tokens by pattern rather than running a calculation.

190
00:16:20,444 --> 00:16:22,883
And it doesn't know when it's wrong.

191
00:16:22,883 --> 00:16:25,118
There's no built-in fact checker.

192
00:16:25,968 --> 00:16:32,348
This is why products like ChatGPT, Claude or Gemini feel more capable than the bare model.

193
00:16:32,348 --> 00:16:35,041
They wrap the language model in tools.

194
00:16:35,041 --> 00:16:37,309
Web search supplies fresh facts.

195
00:16:37,309 --> 00:16:39,861
A code interpreter does exact maths.

196
00:16:39,861 --> 00:16:43,122
Memory systems carry information across chats.

197
00:16:43,122 --> 00:16:49,430
Document retrieval, which we'll build later as RAG, lets it look things up in your files.

198
00:16:49,430 --> 00:16:54,747
When ChatGPT tells you today's weather, the model doesn't know the weather.

199
00:16:54,747 --> 00:16:59,779
It decided to call a tool, read the result, and wrote a reply about it.

200
00:16:59,779 --> 00:17:06,159
The book's analogy: the model is the brain, and the tools give it hands, eyes and a phone.

201
00:17:06,159 --> 00:17:09,845
In part four you'll add these capabilities yourself.

202
00:17:10,695 --> 00:17:15,226
Why do these models sometimes make things up with total confidence?

203
00:17:15,226 --> 00:17:17,931
Because there's no fact database inside.

204
00:17:17,931 --> 00:17:21,041
There are only patterns stored in the weights.

205
00:17:21,041 --> 00:17:26,587
Andrej Karpathy describes a language model as a lossy compression of the internet.

206
00:17:26,587 --> 00:17:31,794
Picture squeezing trillions of words of text into a few gigabytes of numbers.

207
00:17:31,794 --> 00:17:34,499
You can't keep everything word for word.

208
00:17:34,499 --> 00:17:38,691
You keep the gist: grammar, common facts, styles of reasoning.

209
00:17:38,691 --> 00:17:40,247
That's the compression.

210
00:17:40,247 --> 00:17:43,222
But details get lost, that's the lossy part.

211
00:17:44,072 --> 00:17:49,951
When you ask a question, the model is reconstructing an answer from that fuzzy memory.

212
00:17:49,951 --> 00:17:56,172
For common facts, seen thousands of times in training, the reconstruction is usually right.

213
00:17:56,172 --> 00:18:08,340
For rare details, a specific citation, an exact date, the name of a small company's chief executive, there are gaps, and the model fills them with whatever sounds most plausible.

214
00:18:08,340 --> 00:18:12,647
Remember, it was trained to produce likely text, not true text.

215
00:18:12,647 --> 00:18:14,151
Usually those overlap.

216
00:18:14,151 --> 00:18:17,501
Sometimes they don't, and that's a hallucination.

217
00:18:17,501 --> 00:18:22,628
The statistician George Box said all models are wrong, but some are useful.

218
00:18:22,628 --> 00:18:25,772
Language models are useful models of language.

219
00:18:25,772 --> 00:18:29,122
So the rule is simple: verify anything important.

220
00:18:29,972 --> 00:18:31,168
Section five.

221
00:18:31,168 --> 00:18:32,916
Hands-on exercises.

222
00:18:33,766 --> 00:18:36,583
Open any chatbot and try these six prompts.

223
00:18:36,583 --> 00:18:42,281
Ask it to explain photosynthesis like you're five, and watch how it changes vocabulary.

224
00:18:42,281 --> 00:18:47,063
Ask for a haiku about debugging, and check the five seven five structure.

225
00:18:47,063 --> 00:18:52,565
Make it a pirate explaining compound interest, and see whether it holds the persona.

226
00:18:52,565 --> 00:19:01,604
Try the classic puzzle: if five machines make five widgets in five minutes, how long do a hundred machines take to make a hundred widgets?

227
00:19:01,604 --> 00:19:07,368
The right answer is five minutes, because each machine makes one widget in five minutes.

228
00:19:07,368 --> 00:19:09,792
See whether it falls for one hundred.

229
00:19:09,792 --> 00:19:16,342
Ask about yesterday's news, which shows you the difference between the base model and a search tool.

230
00:19:16,342 --> 00:19:18,176
And ask it what it can't do.

231
00:19:19,026 --> 00:19:20,437
Then three exercises.

232
00:19:20,437 --> 00:19:21,915
First, find a mistake.

233
00:19:21,915 --> 00:19:24,333
Ask about something you know deeply.

234
00:19:24,333 --> 00:19:26,684
For me that's software engineering.

235
00:19:26,684 --> 00:19:29,707
Try to catch a subtle error or a fabrication.

236
00:19:29,707 --> 00:19:35,148
This isn't about bashing the technology, it's about calibrating when to trust it.

237
00:19:35,148 --> 00:19:36,357
Second, variation.

238
00:19:36,357 --> 00:19:40,254
Ask the same question in three separate chats and compare.

239
00:19:40,254 --> 00:19:48,247
The answers differ because generation samples from a probability distribution, which we'll implement in chapter eleven.

240
00:19:48,247 --> 00:19:57,047
Third, push the limits: multi-step arithmetic, obscure topics, exact facts, and logic puzzles designed to trip up pattern matching.

241
00:19:57,047 --> 00:20:04,033
If your product has tools, try with and without them, and write down where it shines and where it fails.

242
00:20:04,884 --> 00:20:05,776
Section six.

243
00:20:05,776 --> 00:20:07,486
Why learn to build one?

244
00:20:08,336 --> 00:20:11,169
If ChatGPT already exists, why build one?

245
00:20:11,169 --> 00:20:13,104
The book gives four reasons.

246
00:20:13,104 --> 00:20:14,555
One, demystification.

247
00:20:14,555 --> 00:20:19,046
Magic invites fear and hype, understanding invites good judgment.

248
00:20:19,046 --> 00:20:24,504
Picture a meeting where someone proposes an AI project and everyone nods along.

249
00:20:24,504 --> 00:20:28,926
You'll be the person who can say what's feasible and what isn't.

250
00:20:28,926 --> 00:20:30,447
Two, career advantage.

251
00:20:30,447 --> 00:20:42,055
Understanding how these systems work makes you better in almost any technical role, and that advantage compounds, like understanding the internet early in the nineties.

252
00:20:42,055 --> 00:20:43,506
Three, customisation.

253
00:20:43,506 --> 00:20:53,317
You can fine-tune a model on your own documents, shrink one to run on a phone, or adapt one to a domain or language that big providers ignore.

254
00:20:53,317 --> 00:20:58,016
Four, understanding through building, which leads to the next slide.

255
00:20:58,865 --> 00:21:05,521
Richard Feynman kept a note on his blackboard that said, what I cannot create, I do not understand.

256
00:21:05,521 --> 00:21:14,463
Andrej Karpathy's Zero to Hero video series is built on the same idea, and the author says the book is designed as a companion to it.

257
00:21:14,463 --> 00:21:18,496
I'd recommend watching those lectures alongside this series.

258
00:21:18,496 --> 00:21:26,295
The payoff is a real moment in chapter twelve, when a model you built and trained generates its first coherent text.

259
00:21:26,295 --> 00:21:31,606
That's the difference between watching a magic trick and knowing how it's done.

260
00:21:32,456 --> 00:21:33,517
Section seven.

261
00:21:33,517 --> 00:21:35,336
The checkpoint exercise.

262
00:21:36,186 --> 00:21:37,699
Here's the checkpoint.

263
00:21:37,699 --> 00:21:43,335
Spend fifteen or twenty minutes listing five AI systems you used in the last week.

264
00:21:43,335 --> 00:21:49,935
For each, write what it does, where it sits in the family tree, and what patterns it recognises.

265
00:21:49,935 --> 00:21:55,915
The book gives examples: the spam filter is machine learning, recognising spam signals.

266
00:21:55,915 --> 00:22:01,896
Netflix recommendations are machine learning on your viewing history and similar users.

267
00:22:01,896 --> 00:22:08,358
Voice assistants were classic deep learning, and modern ones increasingly use language models.

268
00:22:08,358 --> 00:22:16,676
Phone autocomplete predicts the next word from the words before it, which, notice, is a tiny version of what an LLM does.

269
00:22:16,676 --> 00:22:18,463
Now find five of your own.

270
00:22:19,313 --> 00:22:21,489
Let's wrap up with the key takeaways.

271
00:22:22,339 --> 00:22:23,333
Six takeaways.

272
00:22:23,333 --> 00:22:29,086
One: AI is pattern recognition at scale, built from statistics, data and compute.

273
00:22:29,086 --> 00:22:37,537
Two: machine learning systems learn by adjusting parameters from feedback, the loop of predict, measure, nudge, repeat.

274
00:22:37,537 --> 00:22:45,988
Three: the hierarchy, AI contains machine learning, which contains deep learning, which contains large language models.

275
00:22:45,988 --> 00:22:55,717
Four: language models can write, code, translate and reason, but on their own they don't have real-time knowledge or reliable arithmetic.

276
00:22:55,717 --> 00:23:00,617
Five: hallucination is a reconstruction error from lossy compression.

277
00:23:00,617 --> 00:23:04,807
And six: the products you use wrap the base model in tools.

278
00:23:05,658 --> 00:23:06,813
Review questions.

279
00:23:06,813 --> 00:23:10,689
Try answering each one out loud before I give the answer.

280
00:23:10,689 --> 00:23:14,225
Question one: what is the simplest definition of AI?

281
00:23:14,225 --> 00:23:20,888
Answer: pattern recognition at scale, finding patterns in data and using them to make predictions.

282
00:23:20,888 --> 00:23:26,055
Question two: how does machine learning differ from traditional programming?

283
00:23:26,055 --> 00:23:29,523
In traditional programming, people write the rules.

284
00:23:29,523 --> 00:23:36,390
In machine learning, we provide data and answers, and the system learns the rules, stored as numbers.

285
00:23:36,390 --> 00:23:38,702
Question three: where do LLMs fit?

286
00:23:38,702 --> 00:23:46,249
They're the innermost circle, deep learning models specialised for language, trained to predict the next token.

287
00:23:47,099 --> 00:23:50,881
Question four: name three strengths and three weaknesses.

288
00:23:50,881 --> 00:23:54,132
Strengths: writing, generating code, summarising.

289
00:23:54,132 --> 00:24:00,436
Weaknesses on its own: real-time information, reliable arithmetic, and knowing when it's wrong.

290
00:24:00,436 --> 00:24:03,422
Question five: why does hallucination happen?

291
00:24:03,422 --> 00:24:15,167
Because the model has no database of facts, its weights are a lossy compression of its training text, and when details are missing it fills the gap with plausible sounding text.

292
00:24:15,167 --> 00:24:20,608
Question six: why understand these systems if you're not going to be a researcher?

293
00:24:20,608 --> 00:24:30,229
Because it gives you sound judgment about what's feasible, a career advantage, the ability to customise models, and intuition for debugging them.

294
00:24:31,079 --> 00:24:32,845
Here's your practice list.

295
00:24:32,845 --> 00:24:37,669
Run the six prompts on two different chatbots and note the differences.

296
00:24:37,669 --> 00:24:44,599
Do the three exercises and keep a little log of when you trusted the model and when you had to verify.

297
00:24:44,599 --> 00:24:46,637
Fill in the five system table.

298
00:24:46,637 --> 00:25:00,089
And for a first taste of code, re-implement the line fit from this video: forty noisy points, start at w four point five and b zero point four, learning rate zero point zero two, four hundred steps.

299
00:25:00,089 --> 00:25:05,728
You should land near w equals three point oh nine and b equals one point seven one.

300
00:25:05,728 --> 00:25:08,582
The code is in the notes for this chapter.

301
00:25:08,582 --> 00:25:12,454
It's my own illustration, not from the book's repository.

302
00:25:12,454 --> 00:25:17,822
Finally, browse the companion repo at github dot com slash FirstLLM slash code.

303
00:25:17,822 --> 00:25:20,268
The notebooks start at chapter five.

304
00:25:21,118 --> 00:25:22,357
That's chapter one.

305
00:25:22,357 --> 00:25:28,165
You now know what AI is and isn't, where language models fit, and why they make mistakes.

306
00:25:28,165 --> 00:25:30,318
But we've stayed at a high level.

307
00:25:30,318 --> 00:25:37,039
Computers only understand numbers, so how do we turn words into numbers in a way that captures meaning?

308
00:25:37,039 --> 00:25:43,368
That's chapter two, how computers understand words, where we meet tokens, vectors and embeddings.

309
00:25:44,218 --> 00:25:49,537
This video is based on the book Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

310
00:25:49,537 --> 00:25:59,202
Everything here is my own adaptation for study, so please support the author and buy the book at leanpub dot com slash First LLM.

311
00:25:59,202 --> 00:26:05,945
The companion code is at github dot com slash First LLM slash code, under the MIT license.

312
00:26:05,945 --> 00:26:09,541
Thanks for watching, and see you in chapter two.

